WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.735
-Our next guest
is a very funny comedian

00:00:02.735 --> 00:00:04.470
whose memoir, "Foolish:
Tales of Assimilation,

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.305
Determination, and Humiliation"
is available now.

00:00:06.305 --> 00:00:08.908
Please welcome back to the show
Sarah Cooper, everybody!

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:10.576
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:10.576 --> 00:00:25.157 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:25.157 --> 00:00:27.927
Welcome to the show
in person this time!

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:29.863
-I'm in the seat.
-Last time you were here,

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:31.697
we talked about
your very popular videos

00:00:31.697 --> 00:00:34.200
where you lip-sync Donald Trump.
-Who?

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.470
-Yeah. I'm wondering, because of
how many of those you did,

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.539
which one of us you think is
more sick of hearing his voice?

00:00:40.539 --> 00:00:43.609
Me or you?
-Um, probably you.

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:45.211
-See, I think it's you
because I --

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:47.847
At least when I do his voice, I
get to make him say what I want.

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:49.449
-Yeah, yeah.
-Where you were, like,

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.918
locked in to, like,
actual things he said.

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:54.120
-Right. It's more terrifying.
-Yeah.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.356
-Right. Yeah. It's, uh --
[ Laughs ]

00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:58.558
The things you're saying
are actually kind of funny.

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:00.960
The things he's saying is --
You are scared.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.695
-Yeah.
-You're just scared. Yeah.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:04.263
-This is --
This is a wonderful book.

00:01:04.263 --> 00:01:06.098
-[ Gasps ] Thank you.
-You're very welcome.

00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:08.768
And a memoir. We actually --
We were talking --

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:11.370
We were at a dinner,
and we were talking
about writing a memoir.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:12.806
And you actually
have some advice

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:15.374
to anyone who would take this
leap into writing a memoir?

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:18.578
-Yes. So, the one thing
I wish I had done

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:21.914
before writing this book
is read a book.

00:01:21.914 --> 00:01:25.652
-Yeah. That's a really smart,
like, step one.

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:27.720
-It's just -- I wish
I had just picked one up.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.523
But, see, the thing is, I skim.
-Yeah, you're a skimmer.

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:32.491
-I'm a skimmer.
I don't really read.

00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:34.961
Who reads? Does anybody read?
You just buy books.

00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:38.965
You don't actually read them.
So that was my first mistake.

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:41.567
And I do want to apologize
to the gentleman

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:43.703
who reviewed the book
on Goodreads

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:47.373
who said, "Sarah admitted that
she wrote most of this high,

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:48.874
and it shows."

00:01:48.874 --> 00:01:50.442
Um...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.442 --> 00:01:52.244
-That seems like
a positive review.

00:01:52.244 --> 00:01:54.046
He's calling you
a truthful person.

00:01:54.046 --> 00:01:56.115
-I am, but should I have
written in the book

00:01:56.115 --> 00:01:57.483
that I wrote this book high?

00:01:57.483 --> 00:01:59.519
Should I have done that?
I don't know.

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:03.756
But I think if you skim it
while high it's really good.

00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:05.458
It's a really good book.
It's a really good book.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:06.993
-That's a very interesting way
of saying it.

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:09.962
"Look. I was high. Don't beat
yourself up. Just skim it."

00:02:09.962 --> 00:02:13.699
-You know, write high,
edit sober, submit drunk.

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:17.036
-There you go. You talk about
your comedy being very --

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:18.438
[ Applause ]
Yes. This is --

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:21.440
This is good memoir advice.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.743
You talk about how your comedy
is very popular with dads.

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:25.345
Could you extrapolate on that?

00:02:25.345 --> 00:02:29.749
-Dads love me.
They love me. They love me.

00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:32.285
Too bad they're married.
-Yeah. Not all of them.

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:34.420
-Not all of them.
Not all of them.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.990
But, yeah,
the first time I got recognized,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.425
I was walking
my little dog, Stella.

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:42.628
She's a cute little Cockapoo.
And she was taking a giant poop.

00:02:42.628 --> 00:02:45.331
And I had my mask on,

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:47.967
and so I bent down
to pick up the poop,

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.236
but I couldn't get
the little green bag open,

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:54.273
so I took down my mask to lick
my fingers to open the bag.

00:02:54.273 --> 00:02:56.809
Very sanitary.

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:59.946
And this little girl,
10-year-old girl,

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:04.216
came over and she said,
"Are you Sarah Cooper?"

00:03:04.216 --> 00:03:07.453
And I was like, "It's happening.
It's happening."

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:10.456
And I was like,
"Yes, I'm Sarah Cooper.

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:12.791
Are you on TikTok?"
[ Laughs ]

00:03:12.791 --> 00:03:16.362
And she was like, "No.
My dad sends me your videos."

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:18.831
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:20.599
Yeah.
-Heartbreaking.

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:23.202
-My dog saw the whole thing.
I mean...

00:03:23.202 --> 00:03:25.071
-Yeah.
-It was really embarrassing.

00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:26.873
And at first,
I was like, you know,

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:28.740
maybe he was just kind of,
you know,

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.643
a younger
sort of millennial dad.

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:32.512
You know, not that bad.
-Sure. Yeah.

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:34.580
-But then I was doing
my Netflix special,

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.749
and Marisa Tomei came over to me
and she said,

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:38.650
"Sarah, can I get
a picture with you?!"

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.487
And I was like, "Oh, my God.
Marisa Tomei wants
a picture with me?!"

00:03:41.487 --> 00:03:43.256
And she was like,
"No, it's for my dad."

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:45.090
-Oh, yeah...
That's how you know.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.126
Once you get
that second example...

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:49.329
-But hello to Marisa Tomei
and her dad.

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:50.630
-And her dad.
-Yes.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:53.066
-You talk about
how you are dating again.

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:55.267
-Yeah.
-You write about your divorce

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:57.236
very openly and honestly.
-Yes.

00:03:57.236 --> 00:03:59.872
-And you're dating again
and that men are....

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:01.507
You find men to be robots

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.209
currently in your
dating experiences,

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:04.911
and you have some red flags.
-I do.

00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:07.346
-Okay. What are some red flags
we should look out for?

00:04:07.346 --> 00:04:09.482
-Tall with a good head of hair.

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:11.017
-Bad? That's bad?

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:14.253
-See, Elon Musk
is building these robots

00:04:14.253 --> 00:04:16.255
because he needs, you know --
he needs little androids

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.491
to build his Teslas.
-Yeah.

00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:19.792
-And so he knows

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:23.062
that a woman will overlook
just about anything

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:25.031
if a man is tall
with a good head of hair.

00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:26.498
-So that is likely a robot.

00:04:26.498 --> 00:04:28.635
-That is likely a robot.
-Okay. Interesting.

00:04:28.635 --> 00:04:31.938
-Short bald men are okay.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:04:31.938 --> 00:04:33.506
-They're okay.
-Yeah.

00:04:33.506 --> 00:04:36.909
-Um, let's see. Monotone voice.
-Yeah.

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:40.012
-Okay? So if he uses
the same tone of voice

00:04:40.012 --> 00:04:42.014
to say, "I went to Duke,"

00:04:42.014 --> 00:04:45.251
as he does to say,
"I'm gonna [bleep]," that's...

00:04:45.251 --> 00:04:46.452
-Yeah.

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:48.154
-That's a robot.
-That's a robot.

00:04:48.154 --> 00:04:49.955
-That's a robot.
Watch out for that.

00:04:49.955 --> 00:04:52.058
-The funny thing
is he doesn't seem excited

00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:53.659
about either of those things.

00:04:53.659 --> 00:04:55.228
-Either of those things.
It's a red flag.

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:56.996
-Yeah.

00:04:56.996 --> 00:05:00.732
-The other one is if he responds
to really cute text messages

00:05:00.732 --> 00:05:02.234
in a weird way.

00:05:02.234 --> 00:05:04.703
Like, if you wake up
thinking about him

00:05:04.703 --> 00:05:06.505
and you send him a text message
and you're like,

00:05:06.505 --> 00:05:08.007
"I woke up thinking about you,"

00:05:08.007 --> 00:05:09.575
and he responds, "LOL."

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.344
Um...

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:13.312
-Classic robot tell.
-Classic robot.

00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:15.448
-So helpful.
This is also very helpful.

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:18.217
You're doing a very kind thing
with your wedding ring.

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:20.919
You're divorced.
You're gonna auction it off.

00:05:20.919 --> 00:05:22.955
-I am.
-For a good cause.

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:24.657
-Yes.
-What is the cause?

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:27.259
-I'm such a good person!
Um...

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:30.062
I, um -- I'm giving it to the
Entertainment Community Fund...

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:32.064
-That's very nice.
-...to auction it off.

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:33.899
-This is helpful
during strike times.

00:05:33.899 --> 00:05:35.768
-Yes, during strike times.

00:05:35.768 --> 00:05:38.937
Because I -- I wanted
to be part of the WGA

00:05:38.937 --> 00:05:41.206
for so long, the Writers Guild.

00:05:41.206 --> 00:05:43.809
And in 2020,
I was actually able to join

00:05:43.809 --> 00:05:47.346
and get my health insurance
through the WGA.

00:05:47.346 --> 00:05:48.947
And without
that health insurance,

00:05:48.947 --> 00:05:50.883
I wouldn't have been able
to get divorced.

00:05:50.883 --> 00:05:52.050
-That's fantastic!

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.419
-So I'm donating
my wedding ring.

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:55.020
-And you can go
to your Instagram page.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.989
-Yes. Go to my Instagram.
-Sarah Cooper, everybody.

00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:58.791
"Foolish" is available
wherever you buy books,

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:00.826
but please support local
and independent book stores.

00:06:00.826 --> 00:06:02.528
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

